        <section id="origins" class="mp-form-card mb-4">
          <h3 class="mb-3">🌊 Origins &amp; Mythology</h3>
          <p>
            The story most closely tied to the Kumbh Mela is that of the <strong>Samudra Manthan</strong>,
            or the Churning of the Cosmic Ocean. According to Hindu mythology, the Devas (gods) and Asuras
            (demons) churned the ocean of milk together in search of Amrita — the nectar of immortality.
            When the Kumbha (sacred pot) containing the nectar finally emerged, a struggle broke out between
            the two sides over its possession.
          </p>
          <p>
            During this celestial tussle, it's believed that a few drops of the nectar spilled at four
            different places on Earth: Haridwar, Prayagraj, Ujjain, and Nashik. These four cities — each
            on the banks of a sacred river — became the sites where the Kumbh Mela is held in rotation,
            said to carry a trace of that divine nectar's blessing.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="cycle" class="mp-form-card mb-4">
          <h3 class="mb-3">🔄 The Kumbh Cycle</h3>
          <p>The timing of each Kumbh Mela is determined astrologically, based on the positions of Jupiter, the Sun, and the Moon. Broadly, the cycle looks like this:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Kumbh Mela</strong> — held every 3 years, rotating among the four sites.</li>
            <li><strong>Ardh (Half) Kumbh</strong> — held every 6 years at Haridwar and Prayagraj.</li>
            <li><strong>Purna (Full) Kumbh</strong> — held every 12 years at each individual site.</li>
            <li><strong>Maha Kumbh</strong> — the rarest and most significant, occurring once every 144 years (after 12 Purna Kumbhs) specifically at Prayagraj, at the sacred confluence — or Sangam — of the Ganga, Yamuna, and the mythical, invisible Saraswati river.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-muted small">Mahapath is built around the Mahakumbh at Prayagraj — the most significant occurrence in this cycle.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="shahi-snan" class="mp-form-card mb-4">
          <h3 class="mb-3">🛁 Shahi Snan (The Royal Bath)</h3>
          <p>
            The <strong>Shahi Snan</strong>, also called <strong>Amrit Snan</strong>, is the most sacred
            ritual bathing event of the Mela. On specific auspicious dates determined by astrology, the
            various Akharas (monastic orders) process to the riverbank in a set order, often with elaborate
            processions, to bathe first — a moment considered deeply auspicious.
          </p>
          <p>
            Millions of pilgrims from across the world join in bathing during these days, believing that
            immersion in the sacred waters at this precise astrological moment cleanses sin and aids the
            soul's journey toward liberation (moksha).
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="akharas" class="mp-form-card mb-4">
          <h3 class="mb-3">🔱 Akharas &amp; Naga Sadhus</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Akharas</strong> are organized monastic orders within Hinduism, some with roots going
            back centuries. Historically, several akharas also maintained martial traditions, formed in
            part to protect pilgrims and sacred sites during turbulent periods in history.
          </p>
          <p>
            Among the most striking sights at any Kumbh are the <strong>Naga Sadhus</strong> — ascetics
            belonging to certain akharas who have renounced worldly life entirely, including clothing, as
            a symbol of complete detachment from material existence. Becoming a Naga Sadhu involves years
            of rigorous spiritual discipline and initiation rites.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="significance" class="mp-form-card mb-4">
          <h3 class="mb-3">🙏 Significance &amp; Beliefs</h3>
          <p>
            For devotees, the Kumbh Mela is far more than a religious festival — it's considered one of
            the most powerful opportunities in a lifetime for spiritual purification. Bathing at the
            Sangam during the Mahakumbh is believed to wash away accumulated sin (paap) and bring one
            closer to moksha, liberation from the cycle of birth and rebirth.
          </p>
          <p>
            Beyond the rituals, the Mela is also a remarkable gathering of scholars, spiritual teachers,
            and pilgrims from every corner of the country and the world — making it as much a confluence
            of culture and philosophy as it is of rivers.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted small mt-3">
            This page offers a general introduction. Beliefs, rituals, and interpretations vary across
            traditions and regions — we encourage deeper reading from authoritative religious and cultural
            sources for a fuller picture.
          </p>
        </section>

